1
00:00:01,456 --> 00:00:07,287
This is Tendero: an AI sales agent that sells, collects and reconciles payments with PayPal,

2
00:00:07,327 --> 00:00:09,585
for small shops that sell over chat.

3
00:00:09,972 --> 00:00:13,160
Across Latin America, small shops sell through chat.

4
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:16,569
The owner answers every message by hand, sends a payment link,

5
00:00:16,609 --> 00:00:20,802
and then checks the PayPal app to guess which payment belongs to which order.

6
00:00:20,842 --> 00:00:23,682
Sales get lost at night, totals get mistyped,

7
00:00:23,722 --> 00:00:26,945
and fake 'I already paid' screenshots slip through.

8
00:00:27,362 --> 00:00:29,513
Tendero puts an agent in that chat.

9
00:00:29,553 --> 00:00:33,286
On the left, a customer of a mate workshop in Buenos Aires.

10
00:00:33,326 --> 00:00:35,338
On the right, the owner's console.

11
00:00:35,378 --> 00:00:40,026
In this recording the agents run on the built-in offline demo brain;

12
00:00:40,066 --> 00:00:43,887
add an API key and Claude drives exactly the same tools.

13
00:00:44,269 --> 00:00:45,783
Lucía writes from Madrid.

14
00:00:45,823 --> 00:00:49,379
The agent calls tools to price the cart and answer about shipping.

15
00:00:49,419 --> 00:00:54,332
Prices come from the server, never from the model, so the total is always right.

16
00:00:54,737 --> 00:01:00,122
Only after she confirms that total can the agent create a PayPal order with the Orders v2

17
00:01:00,162 --> 00:01:00,366
API.

18
00:01:00,406 --> 00:01:05,625
That guardrail lives in code: the agent cannot charge in the same turn it priced the cart.

19
00:01:06,050 --> 00:01:07,363
She approves the payment.

20
00:01:07,403 --> 00:01:10,263
This demo uses our local sandbox simulator,

21
00:01:10,303 --> 00:01:13,837
which speaks the same REST API as the PayPal sandbox.

22
00:01:13,877 --> 00:01:17,280
With sandbox keys, the same code talks to PayPal.

23
00:01:17,690 --> 00:01:18,826
Now the important part.

24
00:01:18,866 --> 00:01:22,352
Tendero captures the order and PayPal sends a webhook.

25
00:01:22,392 --> 00:01:25,243
Tendero asks PayPal to verify the signature,

26
00:01:25,283 --> 00:01:30,368
reconciles amount and currency against the order, records the fee and the net,

27
00:01:30,408 --> 00:01:32,930
and only then tells Lucía she has paid.

28
00:01:33,331 --> 00:01:34,550
What if someone fakes it?

29
00:01:34,590 --> 00:01:37,887
Here, an attacker posts a 'payment completed' webhook.

30
00:01:37,927 --> 00:01:43,028
Signature verification fails, the event is rejected, and no order changes.

31
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:45,131
The owner has a copilot too.

32
00:01:45,171 --> 00:01:46,275
How did we do today?

33
00:01:46,315 --> 00:01:52,129
It runs the reconciliation sweep and answers with real numbers: gross, PayPal fees, net,

34
00:01:52,169 --> 00:01:54,258
and the fake webhook it blocked.

35
00:01:54,659 --> 00:01:55,630
And it can act.

36
00:01:55,670 --> 00:01:58,508
Refund the last order, the gourd arrived cracked.

37
00:01:58,548 --> 00:02:01,833
The agent finds the order and issues a PayPal refund.

38
00:02:01,873 --> 00:02:06,585
When the signed refund webhook arrives, the customer is told automatically.

39
00:02:07,003 --> 00:02:11,758
Under the hood: a provider-agnostic tool loop running Claude Sonnet by default,

40
00:02:11,798 --> 00:02:16,006
PayPal Orders, refunds and webhook verification, an idempotent ledger,

41
00:02:16,046 --> 00:02:18,434
and a sweep that recovers lost webhooks.

42
00:02:18,474 --> 00:02:21,417
Twenty-three automated tests cover these flows.

43
00:02:21,815 --> 00:02:27,055
Tendero is open source under the MIT license and runs locally in five minutes.

44
00:02:27,095 --> 00:02:30,227
Sell while you sleep, and know every payment is real.

